Fix a heap use-after-free in mkCharLenCE() when a string with an
embedded nul needs translating for the error message.

mkCharLenCE() rejects an embedded nul by building a CHARSXP holding
the offending bytes and handing it to EncodeString() to render the
"embedded nul in string" error.  That CHARSXP is not protected.  When
it is marked UTF-8 or Latin-1 and cannot be used as-is in the current
locale, EncodeString() runs it through translateChar(), whose R_alloc()
can trigger a garbage collection that frees the CHARSXP; EncodeString()
then reads it again to compare the translation with the original:

    ==606==ERROR: AddressSanitizer: heap-use-after-free
    READ of size 8 at 0x7e26e2fb0200 thread T0
        #0 R_CHAR               src/main/memory.c:4115
        #1 Rf_EncodeString      src/main/printutils.c:639
        #2 Rf_mkCharLenCE       src/main/envir.c:4782
        #3 ReadItem_Recursive   src/main/serialize.c:2038
       #14 R_Unserialize        src/main/serialize.c:2326
    freed by thread T0 here:
        #1 ReleaseLargeFreeVectors  src/main/memory.c:1167
        #2 RunGenCollect            src/main/memory.c:1951
        #3 R_gc_internal            src/main/memory.c:3238
        #4 Rf_allocVector3          src/main/memory.c:2864
        #6 R_alloc                  src/main/memory.c:2333
        #7 copyAndFreeStringBuffer  src/main/sysutils.c:1817
        #8 Rf_translateChar         src/main/sysutils.c:1834
        #9 Rf_EncodeString          src/main/printutils.c:638
       #10 Rf_mkCharLenCE           src/main/envir.c:4782

Whether the GC runs there depends on the heap state, so the crash is
timing-dependent (ClusterFuzz flagged it as a one-time crasher), but
gctorture(TRUE) makes it deterministic.  In stock R 4.6.1 the freed
block is often reused for the translation itself, and the error then
comes out as "'Rf_getCharCE' must be called on a CHARSXP, but got
'raw'" instead of the intended message; with AddressSanitizer it is a
crash.  The code dates from 2008 (r45435, R 2.8.0), so every release
since is affected.

PROTECT the CHARSXP; the error unwind pops it again.

Found by fuzzing R with AFL++ + AddressSanitizer (OSS-Fuzz unserialize
target, testcase 4802858911072256).  A serialized Latin-1 string of
30000 bytes with a nul poked into it reproduces under gctorture in a
UTF-8 or C locale.

Upstream status: r-devel/r-svn PR #347 (fix plus a regression test in
tests/reg-tests-1e.R, left out here so this carry patch does not go
stale with that file); not yet on R Bugzilla.  Delete this patch once
the fix lands in R trunk (ossfuzz.sh will report it as already-applied
in the meantime, which is harmless).

diff --git a/src/main/envir.c b/src/main/envir.c
index 8cacbbb86f..5321356f36 100644
--- a/src/main/envir.c
+++ b/src/main/envir.c
@@ -4768,8 +4768,10 @@ SEXP mkCharLenCE(const char *name, int len, cetype_t enc)
 	SEXP c;
 	/* This is tricky: we want to make a reasonable job of
 	   representing this string, and EncodeString() is the most
-	   comprehensive */
-	c = allocCharsxp(len);
+	   comprehensive.  It may translate c, and the R_alloc() that
+	   involves can run the GC, so c must be protected: the error
+	   unwind pops it again. */
+	PROTECT(c = allocCharsxp(len));
 	if (len) memcpy(CHAR_RW(c), name, len);
 	switch(enc) {
 	case CE_UTF8: SET_UTF8(c); break;
